  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The analysis relies on historical synthesis and demographic projections rather than new empirical datasets or localized spatial modeling.",
      "what": "This study traces the historical evolution of urban planning across African cities, analyzing how colonial legacies, post-independence policies, and rapid demographic shifts shape contemporary metropolitan structures. It synthesizes planning trajectories to contextualize the projected growth of the continent's urban population to 1.4 billion by the mid-twenty-first century.",
      "why": "It provides a historical framework that explains why current planning paradigms often fail to accommodate rapid demographic growth, shifting the focus from treating African urbanization as an anomaly to understanding it through its specific historical precedents."
    }
  },
